  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Use is not a recommendation",
      "text": "A lump sum can support an immediate income election or a deferred contract, depending on the need. Preserve adequate access to money for near-term expenses and emergencies. Retirement income should be compared with other resources, survivor needs and inflation exposure.",
      "example": "Rosa’s receipt of a lump sum does not establish that the entire sum belongs in a long-surrender-period contract.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.01",
        "6.5.05",
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep the tax arrangement separate from the contract",
      "text": "Eligible individual and group retirement plans can use annuity contracts. An individual retirement annuity is an IRA form. The applicable plan or IRA rules govern contributions, deductions and distributions; the contract governs its insurance benefits. A tax-deferred IRA does not receive extra tax deferral merely from holding an annuity.",
      "example": "A recommendation inside an IRA needs a supportable reason for the insurance features and costs.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.02",
        "6.5.03",
        "6.5.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Tax deferral is not tax exemption",
      "text": "In an eligible personally owned nonqualified annuity, earnings generally are not taxed as they accumulate. Later taxable distributions remain subject to the applicable ordinary-income rules and possible additional tax. Personal nonqualified premiums generally are not deductible.",
      "example": "Calling growth tax-deferred does not mean a withdrawal of earnings is tax-free.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Education funding needs a separate tax and liquidity analysis",
      "text": "A tuition purpose does not itself waive insurer surrender charges or the nonqualified-annuity early-distribution rules. An eligible higher-education exception for an IRA must not be applied indiscriminately to a personally owned nonqualified annuity. Compare the time horizon and actual arrangement; this outline use is not an endorsement of annuities for college savings.",
      "example": "A distribution needed two years from now can conflict with a much longer surrender schedule.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Charitable gift annuities are donor agreements",
      "text": "Under Maryland Insurance §16-114, covered educational or religious organizations, hospitals and community foundations need a special permit before making donor annuity agreements in Maryland. A permitted organization receives money or property in exchange for its promise of annuity payments to the donor or nominee. Eligibility, reserves and filing conditions apply. Do not portray the arrangement as an ordinary commercial insurer contract or promise a fully deductible gift and fully tax-free payments.",
      "example": "Verify the issuing organization and its permit; charitable status by itself is not the permit.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Consumer profile information",
      "text": "COMAR 31.09.12.03 includes age, annual income, financial situation and needs including debts, financial experience, insurance needs, financial objectives, intended use, time horizon, existing assets and financial products, liquidity needs, liquid net worth, risk tolerance including willingness to accept nonguaranteed elements, funding resources and tax status. These factors are evaluated together.",
      "example": "A large account balance does not necessarily mean enough liquid cash remains after purchase.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Care and its limits",
      "text": "The producer exercises reasonable diligence, care and skill, inquires into options available within the producer’s authority, assesses the whole annuity and selected subaccounts and riders, and communicates the recommendation’s basis. The chapter does not require analyzing every product outside that authority, choosing the lowest compensation or automatically providing ongoing monitoring. It does not itself create a fiduciary relationship; separate arrangements can create additional obligations.",
      "example": "A product’s individual advantage does not excuse ignoring its combined costs and restrictions.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Required disclosure is timely",
      "text": "Before recommendation or sale, prominently disclose the producer’s role, authority, insurer relationships, sources and types of cash and noncash compensation, and the right to request more cash-compensation information. On request, disclose a reasonable estimate, potentially as a range or percentage, and whether it recurs and how often. Explain the applicable product features by the required recommendation or sale timing.",
      "example": "Do not wait until after the purchase to first disclose the required relationship and compensation information.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Conflict and compensation rules are related but distinct",
      "text": "Identify and avoid or reasonably manage and disclose material conflicts, including relevant ownership interests. The chapter’s defined material conflict excludes cash and noncash compensation, which is addressed through separate disclosure and supervision provisions. Insurers must eliminate limited-time sales contests, quotas, bonuses and noncash compensation tied to specific annuities; the rule does not prohibit every lawful commission.",
      "example": "An insurer ownership interest can need conflict treatment beyond simply listing a sales commission.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Replacement review covers more than a new bonus",
      "text": "Consider old surrender charges, new surrender periods, benefits lost, increased fees and charges, substantial consumer benefit over the product’s life, and another exchange or replacement—particularly within 60 months. A positive bonus can be offset by larger costs and lost guarantees.",
      "example": "A replacement 48 months ago falls within the stated 60-month lookback consideration.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Documentation and unrecommended transactions",
      "text": "At recommendation or sale, make a written record of the recommendation and basis. Use the required signed acknowledgment for refusal of profile information and understanding its consequences, and for a consumer decision not based on the producer’s recommendation. Defined no-recommendation situations have specific rules; the insurer’s issuance must still be reasonable under circumstances actually known to it.",
      "example": "Do not treat an acknowledgment as a waiver authorizing a recommendation unsupported by the available facts.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Scope, supervision, training and records",
      "text": "COMAR 31.09.12.02 includes defined exceptions such as certain employer retirement-plan transactions and direct-response sales without a recommendation. An individual IRA is not categorically excluded just because it has IRA tax status. The insurer maintains a supervision system and verifies required training. New producers subject to .08 need the approved four-credit training and product knowledge; the old transition provision is not a universal one-credit substitute. Records under .10 remain for seven years after insurer completion and must preserve attachments and signatures in reproducible form.",
      "example": "Completing this exam-prep course does not represent completion of approved annuity continuing education.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Consumers retain complaint and cooperation rights",
      "text": "A producer or insurer must not dissuade a consumer from truthful responses to an insurer’s confirmation request, filing a complaint, or cooperating with an investigation.",
      "example": "A producer cannot tell David to hide his liquidity needs so the recommendation will pass review.",
      "topics": [
        "6.5.08"
      ]
    }
  ],
